WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.698
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.998 --> 00:00:09.083
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.383 --> 00:00:17.190
Unit twenty-seven: Silence and Communication. In this unit, you will explore meaning beyond words.

4
00:00:18.183 --> 00:00:19.521
eloquent silence.

5
00:00:20.221 --> 00:00:24.601
eloquent silence means a silence that communicates a clear message.

6
00:00:24.901 --> 00:00:27.864
His eloquent silence said more than any speech.

7
00:00:28.564 --> 00:00:31.959
Your turn. Say: eloquent silence.

8
00:00:34.659 --> 00:00:36.043
eloquent silence.

9
00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:38.318
unspoken rule.

10
00:00:39.018 --> 00:00:43.003
unspoken rule means a rule everyone follows without stating it.

11
00:00:43.303 --> 00:00:46.417
It's an unspoken rule not to sit in the elder's seat.

12
00:00:47.117 --> 00:00:50.071
Your turn. Say: unspoken rule.

13
00:00:52.771 --> 00:00:53.970
unspoken rule.

14
00:00:54.983 --> 00:00:56.298
tacit knowledge.

15
00:00:56.998 --> 00:01:01.517
tacit knowledge means knowledge gained from experience that is hard to explain.

16
00:01:01.817 --> 00:01:04.745
Riding a bicycle relies on tacit knowledge.

17
00:01:05.445 --> 00:01:08.051
Your turn. Say: tacit knowledge.

18
00:01:10.751 --> 00:01:12.054
tacit knowledge.

19
00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:14.202
implicature.

20
00:01:14.902 --> 00:01:18.806
implicature means what a speaker suggests without saying it directly.

21
00:01:19.106 --> 00:01:22.870
'Some students passed' carries the implicature that not all did.

22
00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:27.116
Your turn. Say: implicature.

23
00:01:29.816 --> 00:01:31.003
implicature.

24
00:01:32.017 --> 00:01:33.180
pragmatics.

25
00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:37.575
pragmatics means the study of meaning in context.

26
00:01:37.875 --> 00:01:42.199
Pragmatics explains why 'Can you pass the salt?' is a request.

27
00:01:42.899 --> 00:01:45.668
Your turn. Say: pragmatics.

28
00:01:48.368 --> 00:01:49.450
pragmatics.

29
00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:51.985
phatic communication.

30
00:01:52.685 --> 00:01:59.863
phatic communication means small talk that builds social connection rather than sharing information.

31
00:02:00.163 --> 00:02:04.290
'Have you eaten yet?' is phatic communication in Khmer culture.

32
00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:08.130
Your turn. Say: phatic communication.

33
00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:12.156
phatic communication.

34
00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:14.569
politeness theory.

35
00:02:15.269 --> 00:02:20.159
politeness theory means a theory of how people protect each other's 'face' in conversation.

36
00:02:20.459 --> 00:02:23.562
Politeness theory explains indirect refusals.

37
00:02:24.262 --> 00:02:27.843
Your turn. Say: politeness theory.

38
00:02:30.543 --> 00:02:31.776
politeness theory.

39
00:02:32.783 --> 00:02:33.970
speech act.

40
00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:40.397
speech act means an utterance that performs an action, like promising or apologising.

41
00:02:40.697 --> 00:02:42.917
Saying 'I promise' is a speech act.

42
00:02:43.617 --> 00:02:46.687
Your turn. Say: speech act.

43
00:02:49.387 --> 00:02:50.469
speech act.

44
00:02:51.483 --> 00:02:53.796
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:54.096 --> 00:03:00.336
Rithy, when someone in Cambodia asks, 'Have you eaten yet?', do they want information?

46
00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:07.904
Not really. Were I to describe my whole lunch, they'd be surprised! It's phatic communication.

47
00:03:09.217 --> 00:03:12.763
A friendly ritual, then. What about silence in meetings?

48
00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:19.849
Silence can be eloquent. Should a junior stay silent, it may signal disagreement, not agreement.

49
00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:25.321
That's where pragmatics helps: meaning depends on context.

50
00:03:26.617 --> 00:03:32.833
And on unspoken rules. Had I spoken directly to my professor, he might have lost face.

51
00:03:34.117 --> 00:03:36.151
Politeness theory in action.

52
00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:43.701
Exactly. Much of our communication relies on tacit knowledge rather than words.

53
00:03:45.017 --> 00:03:48.665
Grammar focus: Conditionals beyond the rules (extended).

54
00:03:48.965 --> 00:03:51.197
Inverted conditionals add polish.

55
00:03:51.497 --> 00:03:58.572
Were I to describe my lunch, they'd be surprised. Should a junior stay silent, it may signal disagreement.

56
00:03:58.872 --> 00:04:02.242
Had I spoken directly, he might have lost face.

57
00:04:02.542 --> 00:04:09.069
Inverted conditionals make spoken English sound polished; use them in presentations and formal discussion.

58
00:04:10.083 --> 00:04:12.817
Now read and listen. What We Don't Say.

59
00:04:13.117 --> 00:04:17.392
Linguists have long recognised that meaning is not contained in words alone.

60
00:04:18.683 --> 00:04:23.643
Pragmatics studies how context shapes what speakers intend and what listeners infer.

61
00:04:24.950 --> 00:04:30.885
Philosopher Paul Grice described implicature: meanings suggested rather than stated.

62
00:04:32.183 --> 00:04:39.883
Phatic communication, such as greetings and small talk, builds relationships rather than exchanging information.

63
00:04:41.183 --> 00:04:46.840
Speech act theory shows that words can perform actions, from promises to apologies.

64
00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:53.749
Politeness theory explains why people soften refusals to protect each other's face.

65
00:04:55.050 --> 00:05:02.100
In many cultures, including Cambodia's, an eloquent silence can be more meaningful than a direct reply.

66
00:05:03.383 --> 00:05:08.169
Were we to listen only to words, we would miss much of what people truly communicate.

67
00:05:09.483 --> 00:05:12.182
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:12.482 --> 00:05:16.606
Small talk that builds connection rather than sharing information is blank.

69
00:05:16.906 --> 00:05:22.858
A, phatic communication. B, implicature. C, speech act.

70
00:05:26.850 --> 00:05:29.511
The answer is A: phatic communication.

71
00:05:31.017 --> 00:05:34.235
blank I to win the lottery, I'd travel the world.

72
00:05:34.535 --> 00:05:37.631
A, If. B, Were. C, Should.

73
00:05:41.617 --> 00:05:43.430
The answer is B: Were.

74
00:05:44.917 --> 00:05:47.056
blank you have questions, please ask.

75
00:05:47.356 --> 00:05:50.382
A, Should. B, Had. C, Were.

76
00:05:54.383 --> 00:05:56.464
The answer is A: Should.

77
00:05:57.950 --> 00:06:01.830
Knowledge from experience that is hard to explain is blank.

78
00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:06.910
A, tacit knowledge. B, pragmatics. C, politeness theory.

79
00:06:10.917 --> 00:06:13.857
The answer is A: tacit knowledge.

80
00:06:15.350 --> 00:06:18.708
According to the text, who described implicature?

81
00:06:19.008 --> 00:06:24.066
A, Paul Grice. B, Edward Hall. C, Sandel.

82
00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:30.676
The answer is A: Paul Grice.

83
00:06:32.183 --> 00:06:37.483
Now it's your turn. Part one: How is meaning communicated without words in your culture?

84
00:06:42.783 --> 00:06:47.836
Part two: Use an inverted conditional; give two examples and explain each.

85
00:06:54.136 --> 00:07:08.203
Much is communicated through silence and tone. Were someone to refuse a request directly, it might seem rude, so people often say 'Let me think about it'. Silence in a meeting, similarly, may express polite disagreement.

86
00:07:08.503 --> 00:07:10.909
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:11.917 --> 00:07:17.065
Great work today! You learned eight new words and conditionals beyond the rules (extended).

88
00:07:17.365 --> 00:07:21.109
Today's value is respect. Thank the people who help us.

89
00:07:21.409 --> 00:07:24.897
See you in Unit twenty-eight. Goodbye!

90
00:07:25.197 --> 00:07:28.163
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

